WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Shake a little soap solution in pure water,

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.640
and you get a tall froth.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.120
Shake the same soap in hard water, and

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.400
it turns cloudy, and the froth barely forms.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.960
A detergent froths in both.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.760
Let's show that fairly, measure it, and explain

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:16.160
it.

00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:20.726
The aim: to compare how a soap and

00:00:20.807 --> 00:00:24.046
a detergent behave in soft and hard water.

00:00:24.407 --> 00:00:26.806
Make hard water by dissolving a known amount

00:00:26.887 --> 00:00:30.086
of calcium chloride in distilled water, with plain

00:00:30.207 --> 00:00:32.486
distilled water as the soft sample.

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:35.126
That adds chloride too, so a soap tube

00:00:35.207 --> 00:00:37.846
in sodium chloride solution is a useful extra

00:00:37.927 --> 00:00:38.726
check.

00:00:38.807 --> 00:00:42.566
Use a pure detergent, like sodium dodecyl sulfate.

00:00:42.647 --> 00:00:45.846
That gives four tubes: soap or detergent, in

00:00:46.047 --> 00:00:47.606
soft or hard water.

00:00:47.687 --> 00:00:49.526
Two more get water only.

00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:50.646
They're the controls.

00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:54.506
Part one is a froth test.

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:57.866
Put ten millilitres of each water into matching

00:00:57.947 --> 00:01:00.426
test tubes, and add five drops of soap

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:01.986
or detergent solution.

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:04.666
Stopper it, shake it ten times the same

00:01:04.747 --> 00:01:06.826
way, and stand it in the rack.

00:01:07.147 --> 00:01:09.946
After thirty seconds, measure the froth height with

00:01:09.987 --> 00:01:12.346
a ruler, from the liquid's surface to the

00:01:12.427 --> 00:01:13.586
top of the froth.

00:01:13.867 --> 00:01:16.506
The controls show how much froth water alone

00:01:16.747 --> 00:01:18.506
makes: almost none.

00:01:20.534 --> 00:01:23.173
What changes, and what stays the same?

00:01:23.654 --> 00:01:27.173
Two independent variables: the cleaning agent, soap or

00:01:27.254 --> 00:01:30.493
detergent, and the water, soft or hard.

00:01:30.774 --> 00:01:33.493
The dependent variable is the froth height, plus

00:01:33.574 --> 00:01:35.093
whether a solid forms.

00:01:35.334 --> 00:01:37.973
Keep the water volume, the drops, the shaking

00:01:38.054 --> 00:01:39.893
and the waiting time the same.

00:01:40.134 --> 00:01:42.693
The two solutions aren't the same strength, so

00:01:42.854 --> 00:01:45.253
judge each agent by how much hard water

00:01:45.334 --> 00:01:46.133
changes it.

00:01:48.134 --> 00:01:50.053
Here are some example results.

00:01:50.374 --> 00:01:53.013
Soap in hard water gives far less froth

00:01:53.094 --> 00:01:55.413
than in soft water, and the liquid turns

00:01:55.494 --> 00:01:57.253
cloudy, with white specks.

00:01:57.734 --> 00:02:00.053
The detergent froths nearly as well in hard

00:02:00.134 --> 00:02:02.613
water as in soft, with little or no

00:02:02.774 --> 00:02:03.733
scum you can see.

00:02:04.294 --> 00:02:07.333
So hard water ruins the soap's froth, but

00:02:07.414 --> 00:02:09.333
barely touches the detergent's.

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:12.053
Why?

00:02:12.294 --> 00:02:15.333
From the soaps video: in hard water, two

00:02:15.414 --> 00:02:18.293
soap ions bind each calcium ion and form

00:02:18.374 --> 00:02:21.653
an insoluble solid, like calcium stearate.

00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:23.573
That's the scum you can see.

00:02:23.654 --> 00:02:26.373
Every soap particle locked in scum is lost

00:02:26.454 --> 00:02:28.213
for frothing or cleaning.

00:02:28.254 --> 00:02:31.093
The detergent's sulfate head forms a calcium salt

00:02:31.174 --> 00:02:33.813
that's much more soluble, so far less scum

00:02:33.894 --> 00:02:36.213
forms, and the detergent keeps working.

00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:41.833
Froth isn't cleaning, so part two tests how

00:02:41.914 --> 00:02:44.233
well each mixture keeps oil spread through the

00:02:44.434 --> 00:02:45.273
water.

00:02:45.354 --> 00:02:47.833
Set up fresh tubes the same way, add

00:02:47.994 --> 00:02:51.033
one millilitre of cooking oil, stopper, and shake

00:02:51.114 --> 00:02:52.473
ten times.

00:02:52.634 --> 00:02:54.313
Then time how long it takes for a

00:02:54.394 --> 00:02:57.273
clear oil layer to reappear at the top.

00:02:57.354 --> 00:03:00.233
In the example results, the oil separates quickly

00:03:00.354 --> 00:03:01.753
with water only.

00:03:01.874 --> 00:03:04.553
With soap in soft water, or detergent in

00:03:04.714 --> 00:03:07.993
either water, it stays cloudy much longer.

00:03:08.074 --> 00:03:10.553
With soap in hard water, it separates much

00:03:10.634 --> 00:03:11.113
sooner.

00:03:13.134 --> 00:03:16.893
That cloudiness is an emulsion: tiny oil droplets

00:03:17.054 --> 00:03:18.333
spread through the water.

00:03:18.654 --> 00:03:21.373
Soap particles coat each droplet, tails in the

00:03:21.454 --> 00:03:23.693
oil and heads in the water, and the

00:03:23.774 --> 00:03:26.133
coating helps keep the droplets apart.

00:03:26.494 --> 00:03:28.733
In hard water, much of the soap is

00:03:28.814 --> 00:03:31.213
locked up in scum, so less is left

00:03:31.294 --> 00:03:33.453
to coat the oil, and the droplets join

00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:34.493
up faster.

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:36.733
When the froth test and the oil test

00:03:36.814 --> 00:03:39.453
point the same way, that makes the conclusion

00:03:39.534 --> 00:03:40.253
stronger.

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:43.226
Is it reliable?

00:03:43.707 --> 00:03:47.066
Repeat each tube three times, or pool results

00:03:47.147 --> 00:03:48.106
with other groups.

00:03:48.667 --> 00:03:50.426
If the froth heights agree within a few

00:03:50.507 --> 00:03:52.986
millimetres, and the order is the same every

00:03:53.187 --> 00:03:55.146
time, the results are reliable.

00:03:55.787 --> 00:03:58.186
If one tube is way off, find out

00:03:58.267 --> 00:04:00.266
what went differently, and report it with your

00:04:00.347 --> 00:04:00.906
reason.

00:04:02.934 --> 00:04:05.653
Hand shaking varies, and the froth depends on

00:04:05.734 --> 00:04:08.213
it, so count the shakes, keep them the

00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:10.933
same length, and have one person shake every

00:04:11.014 --> 00:04:11.893
tube.

00:04:12.054 --> 00:04:14.453
Read every froth at the same time, because

00:04:14.534 --> 00:04:16.213
froth collapses.

00:04:16.294 --> 00:04:19.093
Some detergents contain water softeners, which would hide

00:04:19.174 --> 00:04:21.733
the effect, so use a pure one.

00:04:21.814 --> 00:04:24.693
For accuracy, read the ruler at eye level,

00:04:24.734 --> 00:04:27.013
to the average top of the froth, not

00:04:27.094 --> 00:04:28.133
the tallest bubble.

00:04:30.134 --> 00:04:31.253
Now the risks.

00:04:31.734 --> 00:04:35.333
These chemicals are low hazard, not no hazard.

00:04:35.894 --> 00:04:38.613
The soap, the detergent and the calcium chloride

00:04:38.694 --> 00:04:41.973
solutions can irritate your eyes, so wear safety

00:04:42.054 --> 00:04:42.893
glasses.

00:04:43.174 --> 00:04:45.813
Hold the stopper in firmly while shaking, and

00:04:45.894 --> 00:04:48.413
use tubes without chips or cracks.

00:04:48.774 --> 00:04:51.333
Wipe up spilled oil straight away, because it

00:04:51.414 --> 00:04:53.173
makes the floor slippery.

00:04:53.214 --> 00:04:55.333
And collect the oily mixtures as your school

00:04:55.574 --> 00:04:58.133
directs, not down the sink, where oil can

00:04:58.214 --> 00:04:59.333
block the drains.

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:03.653
One line to keep, from the soaps video.

00:05:04.294 --> 00:05:07.973
Soap makes scum in hard water; detergents mostly

00:05:08.054 --> 00:05:08.613
don't.

00:05:10.634 --> 00:05:11.273
Your turn.

00:05:11.914 --> 00:05:14.073
The table shows how many drops of soap

00:05:14.154 --> 00:05:17.193
solution each water sample needed before a froth

00:05:17.274 --> 00:05:18.713
lasted thirty seconds.

00:05:19.354 --> 00:05:21.833
Explain the result for sample Y, with a

00:05:21.914 --> 00:05:25.033
net ionic equation for one hardness ion, using

00:05:25.114 --> 00:05:26.953
sodium stearate as the soap.

00:05:27.514 --> 00:05:28.873
Pause here to answer.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.700
Sample Y is hard water: it contains calcium

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.460
or magnesium ions.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.020
Stearate ions react with them to form an

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:52.180
insoluble solid, the white scum: calcium stearate, or

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:53.460
magnesium stearate.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.020
So the first drops are used up making

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.540
scum, and froth lasts only once enough hardness

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:00.660
ions are removed.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:04.313
The trap is calling hard water salty: it's

00:06:04.367 --> 00:06:07.940
the calcium and magnesium ions, not sodium chloride.

00:06:09.967 --> 00:06:10.606
Last one.

00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:13.646
A student adds five drops of soap solution

00:06:13.727 --> 00:06:16.446
to hard tap water in one tube, and

00:06:16.527 --> 00:06:19.646
ten drops of a dishwashing detergent to another.

00:06:19.687 --> 00:06:22.686
The detergent makes more froth, and she concludes

00:06:22.927 --> 00:06:25.966
detergents work better in hard water.

00:06:26.087 --> 00:06:29.326
Explain why her conclusion isn't valid, and describe

00:06:29.407 --> 00:06:31.806
two changes that would make it valid.

00:06:31.967 --> 00:06:33.246
Pause here to answer.

00:06:44.467 --> 00:06:47.186
She changed more than the cleaning agent: five

00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:50.226
drops against ten, and a dishwashing liquid that

00:06:50.307 --> 00:06:51.986
may contain softeners.

00:06:52.307 --> 00:06:55.106
Use a pure detergent, test each agent in

00:06:55.307 --> 00:06:57.746
soft and hard water with the same drops,

00:06:57.827 --> 00:07:00.306
and add water-only controls.

00:07:00.427 --> 00:07:03.426
Repeating it is the trap: the unfair test

00:07:03.507 --> 00:07:04.306
stays.

00:07:06.334 --> 00:07:09.053
So: make hard water with a known amount

00:07:09.094 --> 00:07:13.053
of calcium chloride, match the tubes, volumes, drops,

00:07:13.134 --> 00:07:16.893
shaking and timing, include controls, and compare each

00:07:16.974 --> 00:07:19.613
agent with its own soft-water tube.

00:07:19.694 --> 00:07:21.933
Measure froth, and how long the oil stays

00:07:22.014 --> 00:07:23.293
emulsified.

00:07:23.374 --> 00:07:25.800
Calcium locks soap up as scum, while the

00:07:25.827 --> 00:07:29.373
detergent's calcium salt is much more soluble.

00:07:29.614 --> 00:07:31.693
Next: planning a synthesis.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.140
Here are the key notes.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:37.300
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:38.180
to copy them.
